I'll verify this directly via Riemann-type sums and an Abel (summation-by-parts) identity, without invoking the general Itô integration-by-parts theorem.

---

**Direct verification that** $\displaystyle\int_0^t s\,dW_s = tW_t - \int_0^t W_s\,ds.$

Let $\pi_n=\{0=t_0<t_1<\cdots<t_n=t\}$ be a sequence of partitions of $[0,t]$ with mesh $\|\pi_n\|=\max_i(t_i-t_{i-1})\to 0$.

**Step 1 — Two approximating sums.** The Itô integral is the $L^2$-limit of the left-endpoint sums
$$I_n^{\text{left}}:=\sum_{i=1}^n t_{i-1}\,(W_{t_i}-W_{t_{i-1}})\;\xrightarrow{L^2}\;\int_0^t s\,dW_s.$$
Consider also the right-endpoint sums
$$I_n^{\text{right}}:=\sum_{i=1}^n t_i\,(W_{t_i}-W_{t_{i-1}}).$$

**Step 2 — The two sums have the same $L^2$ limit.** Their difference is
$$I_n^{\text{right}}-I_n^{\text{left}}=\sum_{i=1}^n (t_i-t_{i-1})(W_{t_i}-W_{t_{i-1}}).$$
Using independent increments (cross terms vanish) and $\mathbb{E}[(W_{t_i}-W_{t_{i-1}})^2]=t_i-t_{i-1}$,
$$\mathbb{E}\bigl[(I_n^{\text{right}}-I_n^{\text{left}})^2\bigr]=\sum_{i=1}^n (t_i-t_{i-1})^2\,(t_i-t_{i-1})=\sum_{i=1}^n (t_i-t_{i-1})^3\le \|\pi_n\|\sum_{i=1}^n (t_i-t_{i-1})^2\le \|\pi_n\|\cdot t\cdot\|\pi_n\|=t\,\|\pi_n\|^2\to 0.$$
Hence $I_n^{\text{right}}\xrightarrow{L^2}\int_0^t s\,dW_s$ as well.

**Step 3 — Abel (summation by parts) identity.** Apply discrete integration by parts with $a_i=t_i$, $b_i=W_{t_i}$ (note $b_0=W_0=0$, $a_n=t_n=t$, $b_n=W_t$):
$$\sum_{i=1}^n a_i(b_i-b_{i-1})=a_n b_n-a_1 b_0-\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} b_i\,(a_{i+1}-a_i).$$
Substituting,
$$I_n^{\text{right}}=\sum_{i=1}^n t_i(W_{t_i}-W_{t_{i-1}})=t\,W_t-\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} W_{t_i}\,(t_{i+1}-t_i).$$

**Step 4 — Take the limit.** Since $s\mapsto W_s$ is continuous almost surely, the Riemann sum
$$\sum_{i=1}^{n-1} W_{t_i}\,(t_{i+1}-t_i)\;\xrightarrow{\text{a.s.}}\;\int_0^t W_s\,ds$$
(and hence also in $L^2$, since $|W_s|$ is bounded in $L^2$ on $[0,t]$). Passing to the $L^2$-limit in the identity of Step 3 and using Step 2,
$$\int_0^t s\,dW_s \;=\; \lim_{n\to\infty} I_n^{\text{right}} \;=\; t\,W_t-\int_0^t W_s\,ds.$$

Therefore
$$\boxed{\int_0^t s\,dW_s = tW_t-\int_0^t W_s\,ds,}$$
as required. $\blacksquare$

